Z||000000||Loki||10-30-2001||11:08 PM||||OK, I made this pic I really like to be on my main default page.... with links and such. BUT!!! How can I make it my main page so I can make the links work? Its just one big pic and I cant do anything with it. Any ideas?  I have FP2K and Dreamweaver 4.0<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/fieroloki/mainpage.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/fieroloki/loki_sig.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/fieroloki/w4[1].gif"> <BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-31-2001||12:48 AM||||Well, you should probably break that thing apart.  Use a black background and make the images as cropped, transparent, and color reduced as possible.  You can then place them using absolute coordinates and define the parts as hyperlinks.<P>Or use hotspots, but see how long it takes for the thing to download@56k--literally hold your breath...if it is still downloading and you ran out of O2  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">...its too big of a page   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hippocrat||10-31-2001||01:10 AM||||I agree.<P>Use image chunking turn it into small photos then use image mapping to turn the area around the words into links.<P>If you want some good starter info for image mapping and general tips tricks there is some good reading on the site below.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webdeveloper.com/html/html_image_mapping.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webdeveloper.com/html/html_image_mapping.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Hippocrat (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||10-31-2001||02:36 AM||||Yep.........I agree with them both....<P>By using image maps you will make each link word on that page an actual link to another page. basically image mapping denotes the coordinates around the image (think of 360 points around a circle)(in this case a word, My Son, for example), and everything within those coordinates is a "hot spot". The mouse pointer enters the "hot spot" and it makes it a "clickable" image.<P>Also, by dicing the picture into smaller images it will load faster. Basically, it loads smaller images which are then reassembled to form the whole complete image.<P>You really need a simple program like Picture Dicer (it's a freebie I believe) and an image mapping program (I use CuteMap by GlobalSCAPE. Don't ask me why, I have others but I just use that one)<P>And, that is a great page - very tastefully done!!<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||AusFiero||10-31-2001||03:34 AM||||Image Mapping you do need. Look around <A HREF="http://www.tucows.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tucows.com</A>  for an image mapping program. Although I imagine dreamweaver should already have one built in. <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||10-31-2001||10:39 AM||||Jim is right, Dreamweaver does have image mapping.......here's how ya do it...<P>To create a client-side image map: <P>1 Select the image.<BR> <BR>2 Click the expander arrow in the lower right corner of the Property inspector to see all properties.<BR> <BR>3 In the Map Name field, type a unique name for the image map. <P>Note: If you are using multiple image maps in the same document, make sure each map has a unique name. <BR> <BR>4 To define the image map areas, do one of the following:<P>Select the circle tool and drag the pointer over the image to create a circular hotspot. <P>Select the rectangle tool and drag the pointer over the image to create a rectangular hotspot.<P>Select the polygon tool and define an irregularly shaped hotspot by clicking once for each corner point. Click the arrow tool to close the shape. Once the hotspot is created, the hotspot Property inspector appears. (For information about the hotspot Property inspector, see Setting hotspot properties.) <BR> <BR>5 In the hotspot Property inspector's Link field, click the folder icon to browse to the file you want opened when the hotspot is clicked. Alternatively, type the file name.<BR> <BR>6 To set where the linked file opens, type the name of the window in which the file<BR>should open in the Target field. Alternatively, choose a frame name from the Target pop-up menu. <BR> <BR>7 In the Alt field, type the text you want to appear as alternative text in text-only<BR>browsers. Some browsers display this text as a tooltip when the user pauses the mouse pointer over the hotspot.<BR> <BR>8 Repeat steps 4 through 7 to define additional hotspots in the image map.<BR> <BR>9 When you're finished mapping the image, click a blank area in the document to<BR>change the Property inspector. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
